## Final Judgement
The fact-check investigation found no credible polling data from October 2020 in Kentucky that included Kanye West as a presidential candidate. The retrieved evidence addressed general ballot access and a methodology for a student poll in Pennsylvania, but it provided no information about any Kentucky polls, their results, or any subsequent corrections. There is also no indication that any reputable news outlet reported Kanye West leading in such polls.

The specific information still lacking is any evidence of a credible poll conducted in Kentucky during October 2020 that measured voter support for Kanye West alongside Joe Biden and Donald Trump.

Given the complete absence of any supporting evidence from the fact-check, the claim that Kanye West was ahead of Biden and Trump in Kentucky polls in October 2020 is not substantiated. Therefore, the conclusion is that the claim is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The fact-check investigation uncovered no credible polling data from Kentucky in October 2020 that included Kanye West as a presidential candidate. Evidence reviewed addressed unrelated topics, such as [ballot access issues in South Carolina](https://www.postandcourier.com/politics/kanye-west-fails-to-submit-petition-signatures-needed-to-get-on-sc-presidential-ballot/article_e1dd255e-caa1-11ea-b57d-437173ca8e9c.html) and a [student poll methodology in Pennsylvania](https://www.wcupa.edu/sciences-mathematics/mathematics/documents/RamPollReport492021.pdf), but provided no information on Kentucky polls. There was also no indication that any [reputable news outlet](https://www.wsaz.com/2020/09/04/kanye-west-files-to-be-on-presidential-ballot-in-ky/) or [polling aggregator](https://ballotpedia.org/Presidential_voting_trends_in_Kentucky) reported West leading in such polls. Consequently, the claim was refuted due to a complete absence of substantiating evidence.